WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.869 --> 00:00:06.471
You would think with all those furry-hatted
guards wandering around, a team of 24-hour

00:00:06.471 --> 00:00:12.269
security personnel and her own private police
force, whose cars are uniquely painted red

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:15.719
by the way, and let us not forget her very
own guard corgis.

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:20.860
That it would be rather difficult to break
into the home of the world’s largest landowner

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.750
and head-of-state of 16 countries.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:27.550
Surely only a highly trained secret agent
could accomplish such a feat.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:34.269
No, this drunken fellow managed it, without
any prior planning, not once, but twice.

00:00:34.269 --> 00:00:38.329
This paragon of plundering is London-born
Michael Fagan.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:43.050
Ironically he is the son of a champion safe
breaker, but that wasn’t what persuaded

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.890
him to try his hands at unlawfully entering
Buckingham Palace.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:52.620
In interviews he stated he had no motive and
had never even considered the break-in before,

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.239
he had simply gotten drunk at a local pub
and was walking home past the palace at 7am

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:01.519
when he decided to go for a stroll through
the halls of, possibly the world’s most

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:03.280
famous residence.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.110
Unemployed painter &amp; decorator Michael Fagan
made he grand break-in on July 9th 1982 when

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.320
he was 33-years-old.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.210
After leaving the pub, the spritely drunk
managed to scale a 14-foot-high wall at the

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.400
rear of Buckingham Palace’s perimeter.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.640
A wall topped with barbed wire and revolving
spikes nonetheless.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.910
I struggle to climb into bed at 7am after
a night in the pub, so Fagan must have really,

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.430
really wanted to get a glimpse of Queenie
to clamber over that.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:37.480
He then shimmied his way up a drainpipe on
the rear of the palace and entered through

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.330
an unlocked window.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:45.390
Fagan then spent a whole 15 minutes wandering
the halls of the palace without being seen.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.700
One member of the palace’s domestic staff
recalls seeing him but afterwards she said

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:54.610
she didn’t think he was “sufficiently
suspicious” enough to raise an alarm.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:59.060
So presumably one would have to be “outrageously
suspicious” before the staff would actually

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.490
do anything.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:05.670
According to Fagan, after aimlessly wandering
the halls he found his way to the royal apartments

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.990
by “following the pictures” on the walls.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.790
Not really sure what he means by that, but
I can only imagine there are an increasing

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.489
number of photos of corgis on the walls as
you close in on the royal apartments.

00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:23.250
First he entered Charles’ room but he was
not there, Fagan found half a bottle of white

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.159
wine of the shelf which he drunk.

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:30.340
In an interview Fagan later noted that the
wine was “really cheap”.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:35.459
Fagan then snuck into the Queen’s bedroom
where he found Her Majesty asleep.

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:40.310
Fagan later noted that the bed was large but
she was definitely sleeping alone.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.269
He ripped the curtains open and she immediately
awoke.

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:48.730
She sounded her night alarm for help, but
the maid in the next room was vacuuming, so

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:53.290
neither she nor anyone else on duty heard
the Queen’s distress call.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.910
So what did Her Majesty do next, well call
the police of course.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:02.999
I’d just love to be to be the 999 operator
taking a call from the Queen.

00:03:02.999 --> 00:03:07.639
Well she didn’t actually call the police,
she called the palace telephonist who called

00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:10.219
the police for her.

00:03:10.219 --> 00:03:15.469
So what did Fagan and Her Majesty the Queen
get up to for several awkward minutes whilst

00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:18.280
she was waiting for the police to arrive?

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:23.340
Well she, along with a footman, actually lured
Fagan into the pantry with the promise of

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.709
free cigarettes and a glass of whiskey.

00:03:25.709 --> 00:03:30.239
The Queen and her footman kept Fagan busy
in the pantry with cigarettes and whiskey

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:33.779
until the police came and he was arrested.

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:37.439
So what was Fagan’s punishment for his dastardly
royal intrusion?

00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:39.189
A lengthy jail sentence?

00:03:39.189 --> 00:03:41.739
Was he set upon by a pack of ravenous corgis?

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:44.420
Nope, he got away completely scot-free.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.840
Surprisingly, at that time there were no special
laws in place that meant breaking into Buckingham

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.540
Palace was a serious crime, beyond normal
trespassing.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.840
Fagan was let go without any charge because
under UK law trespassing is not a criminal

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.499
offence but a civil wrong.

00:04:02.499 --> 00:04:08.129
The Queen could have taken him to court for
damages, if she wanted to, but can you really

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:10.209
imagine the Queen in court?

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:13.579
She’s far too busy deciding whether she
would like her morning omelette made out of

00:04:13.579 --> 00:04:16.030
quail eggs or duck eggs.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:22.079
The Queen is also exempt from having to legally
provide evidence, unlike us meagre peasants.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:26.270
Fagan was however charged for stealing the
bottle of wine, since that’s the only thing

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.550
they had on him, so why the hell not?

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:33.550
But the theft charge was dropped because the
judge concluded that Fagan was not mentally

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.370
stable and admitted him to spend six months
in a psychiatric hospital.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:43.509
It wasn’t until 2007 when a new law was
introduced in the UK which made trespassing

00:04:43.509 --> 00:04:46.750
on royal or government-owned property a criminal
offence.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:53.259
But this wasn’t Fagan’s first expedition
inside the Palace, he actually broke in a

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:54.830
month earlier too.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:59.710
During his first visit he didn’t meet the
Queen but he spent 10 minutes wandering the

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.870
palace’s vast hallways, staring at paintings.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:07.289
He then found his way to the throne room and
sat on the throne for a bit before leaving

00:05:07.289 --> 00:05:10.479
the palace, completely undetected.

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:15.110
But these two Michael Fagan incidents aren’t
the first times that Her Majesty’s private

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.210
quarters have been penetrated.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:23.060
In 2013 a burglar broke into the palace after
scaling a wall and kicking a door down, he

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.349
made his was deep into the palace but was
swiftly arrested.

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:32.039
Also, in 1981 three German tourists camped
in the grounds of Buckingham Palace the entire

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:34.610
night, mistaking it for Hyde Park.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:41.090
Philip, I think there’s some Germans in
our garden, off with their Hans!

00:05:41.090 --> 00:06:02.820
…I mean heads.

